"John H." <JohnWH@gmail.com> wrote:
>** Why should score go up for killing monsters? **
>
>I'd say it's because that, contrary to the general current of
>opinion here in the newsgroup, the primary goal in Nethack is not
>necessarily to achieve ascension.

Like any game with a defined win-condition, there are two primary goals.
1) Have fun. (Ed: Of course! It's a *game*.)
2) Win.
In the case of Nethack, there are many other goals, some intrinsic, some
extrinsic, but these are secondary goals; they contribute to (1) and
(2) (sometimes in negative ways; being a pacifist makes winning much
harder, for instance, even if it makes the game more fun for some
players), but do not on their own make up the entirety of (1) and (2).

In its present incarnation, the scoring system doesn't work for me.
It doesn't measure how much fun I've had, and it certainly doesn't say
anything about how I've won, although as I'm fairly experienced, I tend
to think a low winning score (and a low winning turn-count) gets one
more bragging rights than a high winning score.  But really, the score
as it now stands only matters if you lose.

(Thinking about turn counts... Hm. It has just occurred to me that given
my increased reluctance to be burdened for any significant amount of
time, it's not a surprise that my ascension turn counts have dropped
from 60-80 to 45-60.)
